      },
      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2011 The Authors Acta Anaesthesiologica Scandinavica © 2011 The Acta Anaesthesiologica Scandinavica Foundation.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "There is some evidence that propofol may reduce post-operative pain. However, the results on the analgesic effects of propofol are inconsistent. Thus, we hypothesized that propofol reduces acute pain if confounding factors like opioids are avoided."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "In this prospective, randomized, subject- and assessor-blind, parallel-group, head-to-head comparative study, 90 American Society of Anesthesiologists I or II females underwent elective laparoscopies were randomized to receive either propofol-based (PR), or sevoflurane-based (SR), or sevoflurane-propofol-based anesthesia (SPR). Pain score at rest assessed by a numerical rating scale at 0.5\u2009h after surgery was the primary outcome. The secondary outcomes included pain score at 1 and 24\u2009h post-operatively, duration of post-anesthesia care units stay (PACU), incidence of post-operative nausea and vomiting, incidence of shivering, and post-operative quality of recovery score (QoR-40) within the first 24\u2009h post-operatively."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "No patients received rescue analgesia. The pain score at 0.5\u2009h post-operatively was less in group PR when compared with group SR (0.7\u2009±\u20091.4 vs. 2.1\u2009±\u20091.8; P\u2009=\u20090.010) or group SPR (0.7\u2009±\u20091.4 vs. 2.1\u2009±\u20092.2; P\u2009=\u20090.008). Group PR was also associated with shorter PACU stay than group SR (21.8\u2009±\u20095.7 vs. 26.2\u2009±\u20096.9; P\u2009=\u20090.050) or group SPR (21.8\u2009±\u20095.7 vs. 27.8\u2009±\u20098.9; P\u2009=\u20090.005). Intraoperative bispectral index values, hemodynamic values and post-operative QoR-40 scores did not differ among the three groups."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Propofol anesthesia was associated with significantly less pain at 0.5 and 1\u2009h after surgery in patients undergoing gynecological laparoscopies with planned opioid-free post-operative analgesia."
          }
        ]
      },
